#include "globals.h"

/* Per-speed-bucket turn-rate table at 0x8006c5f0; row stride 0x1a bytes, the
   lean-indexed rate short sits at +0xc (short[6]). */
extern short D_8006C5F0[];

extern void IntegrateSpyroBodyEuler(int delta);
extern int AbsAngleDelta12(int a, int b);

/* 0x8003d6d0: Steers Spyro's body yaw toward the move-target yaw using a lean
   accumulator g_nSpyroBodyYawStep (clamped -6..+6). Picks the turn rate from
   the 2D table (speed bucket selects the row, the lean the column). Resets the
   lean when the desired delta falls inside the table-indexed band, then
   integrates the chosen angular delta and reports the remaining |delta| via
   AbsAngleDelta12. */
/* PARKED 2026-08-11-3 at 2/92 (97.8%), length- and schedule-exact — was 18/92.
   Re-derived from scratch (the 2026-07-25 permuter body is gone); three of the
   four old residues fell:
     - the shared `iVar1` constant local was an artifact (§G rule 1): writing
       `g_nSpyroBodyYawStep = 6;` / `= -1;` per arm and letting jump.c
       cross-jump the `sw` tail puts the constant in v0 as the original does,
       and removes the pseudo whose live range conflicted with v0;
     - the missing `move a2,a0` is NOT a register tie: the param only has to
       leave a0 because the table `la` is emitted BEFORE the *0x1a ladder.
       Splitting the base into its own statement (`base = D_8006C5F0;`) moves
       the la up and the copy appears by itself (A139/A164);
     - the a1<->a2 role swap between the param copy (4 refs / 34 insns) and
       `delta` (4 refs / 41 insns) is an A176 ref-count dial: wrapping the whole
       step if/else in `do { } while (0)` weights delta's compare ref twice
       (flow.c adds loop_depth per ref), taking it to 5 refs / pri 0.244 vs the
       param's 0.235, so delta is allocated first and takes a1.

   SOLE RESIDUE (2 insns), and it is a proven local-alloc tie, not a schedule:
     orig  subu a1,v0,v1 ; andi a1,a1,0xfff
     ours  subu v0,v0,v1 ; andi a1,v0,0xfff
   The original's subu writes the `delta` pseudo directly, which only happens
   when the AND is its own statement (`delta = t - b; delta = delta & 0xFFF;`):
   local-alloc's combine_regs refuses to tie the and's input to a multi-block
   pseudo (`reg_qty[sreg] == -1`), so in the one-statement form the subu's temp
   ties to the first dying input instead and lands in v0.
   But writing the split form costs the LOAD ORDER (measured, 5/92): with the
   subu's dest no longer a local temp, the two loaded temps are ranked by
   local-alloc.c `qty_compare_1` = floor_log2(R)*R*size/(death-birth), and with
   R=2 each the SECOND load (shorter range: 2 vs 3 insns, the param copy sits
   between) always wins v0. Declaring the body load first fixes the registers
   and costs the emitted order instead (4/92, sched1 keeps source order for the
   pair). The tie is reachable only at exactly R=3 on the first-loaded temp
   (3/3 == 2/2 → qty_compare_1 falls through to `*q1 - *q2`, lower qty number =
   born first = the target load), and the only ref dial we have (A176) needs a
   `do { } while (0)` around that ONE statement, whose loop note splits the
   basic block, evicts `move a2,a0` from the bubble and costs +1 insn (measured:
   fails to link). No other source form adds exactly one weighted ref.
   Class: F1 local-alloc qty_compare tie with a numeric criterion (R=3 on the
   first-loaded temp) — permuter-or-never; do not re-derive the above.

   Re-tested 2026-08-14, seven more forms, all confirming the above:
     - `t = t - b; delta = t & 0xFFF;` (subu into the loaded temp), a
       block-scoped `m` for the masked value (`m = (t-b) & 0xFFF; delta = m;`)
       and its split variant (`m = t-b; m = m & 0xFFF; delta = m;`) are all
       BYTE-IDENTICAL to the one-statement form: combine_regs ties the subu's
       dest to the first dying input regardless of whether the mask's dest is
       block-local, so making `delta` single-block does not reach it.
     - `b = t - b; delta = b & 0xFFF;` is 6/92 and `delta = tgt; delta -= body;
       delta &= 0xFFF;` is 9/92 (both worse).
     - the split form plus a `do { } while (0)` ref dial on EITHER load costs
       the length (92/92, +1 insn) exactly as predicted — the loop note splits
       the block and evicts the `move a2,a0` bubble-filler. Wrapping BOTH loads
       is useless by construction (the ratio is what decides, and both refs
       scale together).
   Re-confirmed 2026-08-18-1 with the real `-dl` numbers (do not re-measure):
   in the split/target-first form the two loaded temps are `b` = 2 refs / 4
   insns (priority 1*2/4 = 0.5, wins v0) and `t` = 2 refs / 6 insns (0.333).
   R=3 on `t` gives 3/6 = 0.5, an exact tie that `qty_compare_1` breaks on qty
   number -- and `t` is born first, so it wins.  The A176 dial around the target
   load ALONE was re-run: still +1 insn (fails to link), same eviction.
   NEW this session, and also a dead end -- the "both global" route.  Giving
   BOTH loads a second set (reusing them for the in-`if` recomputation) moves
   them out of local-alloc, and then:
     - only `t` multi-set: the emitted ORDER comes out right (a de-boosted def
       drifts to the front of the block) and the subu's dest is right, but `b`
       is still block-local so local-alloc hands it v0 first -> 10/92;
     - both multi-set, target first in source: order right in BOTH blocks, subu
       dest right, and the entire residue is the v0/v1 swap -> 10/92. global.c
       `allocno_compare` is the same floor_log2(R)*R/live_length formula, and
       `t` is 4 refs / 6 vs `b` 4 refs / 4, so `b` wins again.
   The invariant behind all of it: whichever temp is EMITTED first is BORN
   first, so its live range is always the longer one, so at equal ref counts it
   always loses.  R is the only free variable in either allocator, which is why
   the R=3 criterion below is the whole problem.
   The live lengths make this unreachable by range dials too: the first-loaded
   temp's range is strictly longer than the second's, so any insn added between
   the second load and the subu lengthens both. R is the only free variable.

   Screened 2026-08-27 against A231 (the base-symbol alias ref dial that closed
   two frames the same session) and it does NOT reach this: A231 works by giving
   two reads of one location DIFFERENT base symbols so cse stops value-numbering
   them, which SPLITS a pseudo and can only LOWER R. The criterion here needs
   R RAISED to exactly 3 on the first-loaded temp, so aliasing is the wrong
   direction; and the merge direction (aliasing the body global in the in-`if`
   recomputation so only `t` picks up the third ref) is the "only `t` multi-set"
   case already measured above at 10/92. Still permuter-or-never. */
void TurnSpyroTowardMoveTargetWithLean(int bucket) {
  int delta;
  int rate;
  short *base;
  short *row;

  delta = (g_nSpyroMoveTargetYaw - g_nSpyroBodyYaw) & 0xFFF;
  do {
    if (delta < 0x800) {
      g_nSpyroBodyYawStep = g_nSpyroBodyYawStep + 1;
      if (g_nSpyroBodyYawStep < 1) {
        g_nSpyroBodyYawStep = 1;
      }
      if (g_nSpyroBodyYawStep >= 7) {
        g_nSpyroBodyYawStep = 6;
      }
    } else {
      g_nSpyroBodyYawStep = g_nSpyroBodyYawStep - 1;
      if (g_nSpyroBodyYawStep < -6) {
        g_nSpyroBodyYawStep = -6;
      }
      if (g_nSpyroBodyYawStep >= 0) {
        g_nSpyroBodyYawStep = -1;
      }
    }
  } while (0);

  base = D_8006C5F0;
  row = (short *)((char *)base + bucket * 0x1a);
  rate = row[g_nSpyroBodyYawStep + 6];
  if (rate >= delta || rate + 0x1000 <= delta) {
    rate = (g_nSpyroMoveTargetYaw - g_nSpyroBodyYaw) & 0xFFF;
    if (rate > 0x800) {
      rate = rate - 0x1000;
    }
    g_nSpyroBodyYawStep = 0;
  }
  IntegrateSpyroBodyEuler(rate);
  AbsAngleDelta12(g_nSpyroMoveTargetYaw, g_nSpyroBodyYaw);
}
